# What Prettier owns in this repository, and what it does not.
#
# Everything not listed here is formatted and checked, including the Markdown
# at the root and the workflow files. Before this file existed Prettier ran
# only from site/, so a README edit went through no formatter at all, and the
# first person to run it by hand reformatted twenty-eight lines of a table that
# had never been through one.
#
# This is gitignore syntax: a # only starts a comment at the beginning of a
# line. A trailing one is part of the pattern and silently matches nothing.

# --- Not text this repository writes ---
# .git holds rerere caches with conflict markers in them, which no parser can
# read; with a directory argument Prettier walks into it.
.git/
node_modules/
site/node_modules/
site/dist/
site/.astro/
pnpm-lock.yaml
plan/
# Scratch directory some local tooling creates. Not part of the repository and
# not in git, but it is in the working tree, so the linters find it.
.superpowers/

# --- Written by a generator, byte for byte, and held to it by a check ---
# Two authorities over one file means whichever ran last wins, and only one of
# them can read the Go types the file comes from.
# cmd/gen_dashboards, checked by make check-dashboards
dashboards/
# cmd/gen_compose, checked by make check-compose
deploy/compose.*.yaml
# site/scripts/gen-docs.mjs, checked by pnpm docs:check
docs/
# cmd/gen_layouts, checked by make check-layouts
site/src/data/layouts.json
# cmd/gen_config, checked by make check-config-options
site/src/data/config-options.json
# cmd/gen_brand
brand/

# --- Fixtures: what GitHub actually answered ---
# API responses kept as they arrived, so that a diff against what GitHub sends
# is a diff and not a reformat. One of them, rate_limit.json, is not even valid
# JSON: it carries @SOON_EPOCH@ where a number goes.
test/e2e/testdata/
test/e2e/docker/out/
internal/collect/testdata/
internal/config/testdata/

# --- Formatted by hand, on purpose ---
# The workflows carry comments that explain the job graph and line up with it,
# and actionlint is their linter. The compose file the containerized suite runs
# is the same kind of file. Reformatting either buys consistency and costs the
# shape somebody wrote deliberately.
.github/workflows/
test/e2e/docker/docker-compose.yml
# The example configuration is documentation that happens to parse: its
# comments sit where they explain the key below them.
config.example.yaml

# --- Owned by another formatter ---
# .astro is handled by astro check and eslint; no prettier-plugin-astro here.
*.astro
# The documentation content is Markdown in an MDX envelope and markdownlint
# owns it. Prettier reflowed a fenced code block nested in a <TabItem> onto its
# own opening fence line, which rendered the quickstart's install tab as an
# empty code block; two authorities over one file made each formatter dirty the
# other.
site/src/content/docs/**/*.mdx
